<main id="main">
<section class="phero"><img class="phero-bg" src="/assets/img/photos/livingroom.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/livingroom-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/livingroom.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="phero-shade"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">›</i><span aria-current="page">Ask an appraiser</span></nav><p class="kicker">Answers by topic</p><h1>Insurance Appraisal Questions from New York Owners</h1><p class="lede">Plain answers to what we hear most about the appraisal process, costs, deadlines and New York’s rules.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-orange" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-l" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><nav class="jump" aria-label="FAQ topics"><a href="#faq-0">Appraisal basics</a><a href="#faq-1">Costs and fees</a><a href="#faq-2">Timing and deadlines</a><a href="#faq-3">New York rules and agencies</a><a href="#faq-4">Working with us</a></nav><h2 id="faq-0">Appraisal basics</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What is an insurance appraisal?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It is a procedure in most property policies for settling disagreements over the amount of a covered loss. Each side names an appraiser, the appraisers choose an umpire, and an award signed by any two of the three sets the amount.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is an insurance appraiser the same as a real estate appraiser?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. A real estate appraiser estimates market value for a sale or mortgage. An insurance appraiser values the cost to repair or replace damaged property under the terms of an insurance policy.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can appraisal decide whether my claim is covered?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Generally not. Appraisal decides the amount of loss, while coverage questions are usually for the insurer, DFS complaints or a court. If your claim was denied outright, talk to a New York attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who can demand appraisal?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Under the New York standard form and most policies modeled on it, either the policyholder or the insurer can demand appraisal in writing. Check your policy for any conditions that come first.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is the appraisal award binding?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Under the standard form, an award signed by two of the three panel members determines the amount of loss. Courts may set an award aside only in limited situations, such as fraud or a panel exceeding its authority.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-1">Costs and fees</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who pays for the appraisal?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Under the standard form, each side pays its own appraiser, and the umpire’s fee and other appraisal expenses are shared equally. Your policy may word this differently, so check it.</p></div></details><details><summary>How are appraiser fees structured?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Arrangements vary and can include hourly, flat or other fee structures. Some carriers question whether an appraiser paid on a percentage of the award is disinterested, so ask how any appraiser is paid and get it in writing.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is appraisal worth it for a small difference?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Sometimes not. When the gap is modest, appraiser and umpire fees can absorb much of the increase. Our settlement gap and fee estimator can help you test the numbers before you decide.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I recover my appraisal costs from the insurer?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Generally, appraisal does not shift fees to the other side. Whether any costs are recoverable in a particular situation is a legal question for a New York attorney.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-2">Timing and deadlines</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>How long does appraisal take?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It depends on the size of the loss and how quickly both appraisers engage. Simple residential disputes can resolve in weeks, while complex or commercial claims can take several months.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is there a deadline to demand appraisal?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Some policies set one and many do not, but other deadlines still apply. Many New York property policies limit lawsuits to two years from the loss, so check your policy and talk to an attorney if that date is near.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal stop the suit-limitation clock?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not necessarily. Whether appraisal pauses a contractual deadline depends on the policy and the circumstances, so do not assume it does. Ask a New York attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long do the appraisers have to pick an umpire?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The New York standard form gives them 15 days, after which either party can ask a judge to appoint one. Your policy may use similar or different time frames.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-3">New York rules and agencies</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Who regulates insurers in New York?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The New York State Department of Financial Services (DFS) licenses insurers and public adjusters and accepts consumer complaints. Its claim-handling standards are found in Regulation 64.</p></div></details><details><summary>What is the New York Standard Fire Policy?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It is the statutory fire policy form set out in Insurance Law §3404, adopted in 1943. Its appraisal provision became the model for the appraisal clauses in most U.S. property policies.</p></div></details><details><summary>What is NYPIUA?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The New York Property Insurance Underwriting Association is the state’s FAIR Plan, a residual market insurer for property owners who cannot find coverage in the regular market. Its policies have their own terms, so read the appraisal wording in yours.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does a homeowners policy cover flood in New York?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Generally no. Flood is excluded from standard homeowners policies; flood coverage usually comes from the National Flood Insurance Program or a private flood policy, each with its own terms.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can I file a complaint about how my claim is being handled?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes, DFS accepts consumer complaints about insurers. A complaint is separate from appraisal and does not set the amount of loss.</p></div></details></div><h2 id="faq-4">Working with us</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Are you a law firm or public adjuster?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. We are independent insurance appraisers. We do not give legal advice and we do not act as public adjusters; our work focuses on valuing the amount of loss.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you work for insurance companies?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>When you retain us as your appraiser, we work for you. We may also serve as neutral umpires, and in that role we work for neither side.</p></div></details><details><summary>Where in New York do you work?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>We take appraisal assignments across New York State, from the five boroughs and Long Island to the Hudson Valley, Capital Region, Central New York and Western New York.</p></div></details><details><summary>What should I send for an initial review?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Your policy and declarations page, the carrier’s estimate and payment letters, any contractor estimate, and photos of the damage. That is usually enough for us to tell you whether appraisal looks worth pursuing.</p></div></details><details><summary>How do I get started?<span class="pm" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Call +1 (844) 537-1427 or email info@newyorkinsuranceappraiser.com, Monday to Friday, 8am to 6pm ET. We will explain our fee arrangement in writing before you decide anything.</p></div></details></div></article>
<aside class="side"><div class="side-sticky"><div class="side-box side-call"><p class="side-t">Talk it through</p><p>A first conversation is free. We’ll tell you honestly whether appraisal fits your claim.</p><a class="side-tel" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg>(844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-orange btn-block" href="/reach-us/">Send claim details</a></div><nav class="side-box side-links" aria-label="Resources"><p class="side-t">Resources</p><ul><li><a href="/appraisal-walkthrough/">Appraisal walkthrough</a></li><li><a href="/ask-an-appraiser/" aria-current="page">Ask an appraiser (FAQ)</a></li><li><a href="/claim-vocabulary/">Claim vocabulary</a></li><li><a href="/policyholder-guides/">Policyholder guides</a></li></ul></nav></div></aside></div><section class="cta-strip"><div class="wrap"><div><h2>Didn’t see your question?</h2><p>Call or email. We’re happy to talk it through.</p></div><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-orange" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-l" href="/reach-us/">Email the estimate</a></div></div></section>
